## Abstract

This dissertation introduces the Civilizational AI Power Index (CAPI), a rigorous and self-contained mathematical framework for quantifying, comparing, and projecting the aggregate artificial intelligence strategic capacity of nation-states. The framework formalizes multi-dimensional dependencies between computational hardware, energy infrastructure, semiconductor sovereignty, research capital, and software ecosystem depth as a generalized Cobb-Douglas production functional, establishes formal axiomatic foundations with explicit discussion of each assumption's contestability, derives closed-form expressions for national AI capability trajectories, integrates compute-optimal scaling laws at the nation-state level of analysis, and develops a two-nation dynamical system admitting competitive and cooperative bifurcation regimes. The Alliance Acceleration Theorem is proved formally. A stochastic jump-diffusion extension of the CAPI dynamics is specified. A complete sensitivity analysis of the convergence time estimator establishes its structural fragility and motivates the probabilistic replacement. Every numerical value in this work is explicitly an heuristic prior, not an empirical result. Every methodological gap in the framework has been mapped to a peer-reviewed solution, specified here as a prescriptive protocol for future researchers. The dissertation concludes with a complete four-phase empirical implementation program. Strict epistemological separation between theoretical claims, empirical claims, and philosophical claims is maintained throughout. No experiments, computational training runs, data analyses, or econometric estimations have been executed. The theoretical architecture and the empirical roadmap stand as independent contributions.

