## Chapter 8 — The Energy-Compute Nexus: Structural Constraints and Escape Velocities

### 8.1 The Binding Energy Constraint

The International Energy Agency reports that global data center electricity consumption reached approximately 415 TWh in 2024, growing at 12% per year over the preceding five years. Updated projections show data center consumption roughly doubling from 485 TWh in 2025 to approximately 950 TWh by 2030, with AI-focused data center consumption tripling over the same period. RAND's power demand projections estimate AI data centers required approximately 21 GW in 2025, with potential requirements of 68 GW by 2027 and 327 GW by 2030 — comparable to California's entire 2022 electricity generation capacity. These projections motivate the formal treatment of energy as a binding constraint on compute expansion.

### 8.2 The Power Stress Index

**Definition 8.1 (Power Stress Index) (T)**: The Power Stress Index for a geographic region ρ at time t is:

PSI(ρ, t) = D_AI(ρ, t) / C_grid(ρ, t)

where D_AI is AI data center power demand and C_grid is total grid generation capacity. Regions in which PSI exceeds 0.25 — the critical threshold beyond which AI infrastructure expansion risks triggering power rationing and construction freezes — include Northern Virginia, Oregon's Willamette Valley, and Western Ireland under current trajectory projections.

### 8.3 The Efficiency Escape Velocity

**Definition 8.2 (Maximum Sustainable Compute) (T)**: The maximum sustainable compute capacity of a nation given fixed energy supply E_available is:

C_max(N, t) = E_available(t) · η / ρ_hardware(t)

where ρ_hardware is the energy-per-FLOP ratio of the deployed hardware generation, declining over time as efficiency improves.

**Proposition 8.1 (Efficiency Escape Velocity) (T)**: If hardware energy-per-FLOP ρ_hardware decreases by a factor of 2 every τ years, then C_max doubles every τ years even with constant energy supply.

**Proof**: C_max ∝ 1/ρ_hardware. If ρ_hardware(t) = ρ_0 · 2^{−t/τ}, then C_max(t) = C_max(0) · 2^{t/τ}. □

The empirical rate of AI accelerator efficiency improvement has been approximately doubling every 18–24 months since 2015, suggesting τ ≈ 1.5–2 years. This provides a structural path to compute growth independent of energy expansion, but only as long as hardware efficiency improvements continue at the historical rate. Open Problem 12.5 asks whether energy ultimately becomes the unique binding constraint as compute demand growth outpaces efficiency escape velocity — a question that becomes tractable once the empirical exponent vector is available.

