## Chapter 9 — Stochastic Extension: The CAPI Jump-Diffusion Process

### 9.1 Motivation for Stochastic Treatment

The deterministic CAPI dynamical system is a necessary idealization for analytical tractability but is an inadequate representation of reality over strategic planning horizons. Real geopolitical systems experience continuous stochastic perturbations from unexpected breakthroughs, hardware yield surprises, policy shifts, and energy price volatility, as well as discrete jump shocks from export control announcements, major model releases, energy infrastructure decisions, and alliance restructurings. A scientifically honest treatment of convergence timing and AI power trajectory uncertainty requires a stochastic framework.

### 9.2 The CAPI Stochastic Differential Equation

**Definition 9.1 (CAPI Jump-Diffusion SDE) (T)**:

d(CAPI_t) = g · CAPI_t dt + σ_0 · CAPI_t dW_t + J_t dN_t

where g · CAPI_t is the deterministic drift term; σ_0 · CAPI_t is the diffusion coefficient representing proportional volatility under Geometric Brownian Motion structure; W_t is a standard Wiener process; J_t is the jump size random variable with distribution F_J; and N_t is a Poisson counting process with intensity λ_jump representing the arrival rate of discrete shocks.

**Proposition 9.1 (T)**: Under the GBM component in the absence of jumps, the CAPI at time t conditional on CAPI_0 is log-normally distributed: ln(CAPI_t / CAPI_0) ~ N[(g − σ²/2)t, σ²t], giving a 95% confidence interval:

CAPI_t ∈ [CAPI_0 · exp((g − σ²/2)t − 1.96σ√t), CAPI_0 · exp((g − σ²/2)t + 1.96σ√t)]

In the full jump-diffusion model, the expected convergence time becomes:

E[T*] = ln(r) / (g_CN − g_US − σ²_effective/2)

where σ²_effective accounts for the additional volatility in the growth differential. Critically, the variance of T* may be infinite if σ is sufficiently large relative to the drift differential — a formal mathematical statement that convergence time forecasts are fundamentally unreliable under geopolitical uncertainty with realistic volatility parameters.

### 9.3 Jump Shock Classification and Prescribed Parameter Estimation

Five categories of shocks relevant to CAPI dynamics are identified. Positive compute jumps arise from major new chip generations or data center announcements. Negative semiconductor shocks arise from new export control packages reducing S(N, t) discontinuously. Positive algorithmic shocks of the DeepSeek type effectively increase Λ(t) and reduce the effective compute cost of capability acquisition. Energy infrastructure shocks arise from power grid failures or policy-driven construction freezes. Geopolitical discontinuities encompass alliance changes, conflict escalation, and technology decoupling acceleration.

The prescribed estimation protocol for jump parameters proceeds in two stages. In the first stage, the Lee-Mykland jump detection statistic is applied to high-frequency proxy series — weekly chip shipment data, monthly policy announcement counts, quarterly benchmark performance indices — to identify statistically significant discontinuities in the time series and estimate their timing and magnitude from the data directly. In the second stage, the Bayesian Hierarchical Poisson-Gamma model is fitted to the detected event record, with partial pooling across shock categories enabling sparse data in less-observed categories to borrow strength from better-documented ones. The specification is:

ln λ_{kt} = μ_k + α_k' X_t + η_k

where X_t includes Wassenaar Arrangement meeting cycles as instruments for export control timing, US Congressional election cycles as instruments for policy acceleration, and Chinese Communist Party Politburo Standing Committee meeting cycles as instruments for domestic industrial policy shocks. Posterior distributions over (λ_k, F_{J,k}) for all five shock categories are then integrated into the Monte Carlo simulation of the full SDE system.

### 9.4 The Monte Carlo Simulation Program

The complete stochastic analysis requires the following simulation protocol, which is mandated in Phase III of the empirical calibration. Initialize state vectors for both nations from the uncertainty distributions of Chapters 4 and 5, drawing the initial power ratio r and dimensional growth rates from their estimated posterior distributions. Simulate 10,000 trajectory pairs forward to t = 50 years using the SDE system with calibrated jump parameters, recording the convergence time T* for each trajectory pair where finite convergence occurs within the simulation horizon. Report the full distribution of T*, including expected value, variance, interquartile range, and the probabilities P(T* ≤ 10), P(T* ≤ 20), P(T* ≤ 30), and P(T* ≤ 50). This distributional output is the scientifically valid replacement for the single-point deterministic T* estimate of Chapter 6.

