## Chapter 11 — Civilizational Acceleration: Extended Theoretical Framework

### 11.1 The Civilizational Acceleration Coefficient

**Definition 11.1 (Civilizational Acceleration Coefficient) (T, P)**: The Civilizational Acceleration Coefficient of a nation-state N at time t is:

CAC(N, t) = Ψ · CAPI(N, t)^θ · K(N, t)

where Ψ is a civilization-specific base productivity constant, θ > 1 captures superlinear returns to AI capability in civilizational expansion, and K is the physical capital stock available for deployment.

The assumption θ > 1 is (P) — a philosophical-scientific conjecture motivated by general-purpose technology theory in the tradition of Bresnahan and Trajtenberg (1995), which argues that technologies that reduce the cost of innovation across all sectors generate returns exceeding the sum of their sectoral applications. The conjecture has not been empirically established for AI specifically and constitutes a research priority for future quantitative work.

### 11.2 Alliance Amplification Under Superlinear Returns

Under cooperation with Γ_coop ≈ 1.375 and θ = 1.5:

CAC_Alliance / CAC_US = [Γ_coop · (CAPI_US + CAPI_CN) / CAPI_US]^θ = [1.375 · (1 + 1/r)]^{1.5} ≈ 2.91 (T)

Across the full range of plausible θ ∈ [1, 2], the cooperation multiplier on civilizational acceleration is bounded below by approximately 2× (at θ = 1, linear returns) and above by approximately 4× (at θ = 2, strongly superlinear returns). This robust lower bound — that cooperation under any plausible assumption about AI's civilizational role generates at least twice the acceleration of competitive unilateral development — is the Alliance Acceleration Theorem's ultimate substantive conclusion. The interpretation of this conclusion as a policy argument is (P) and lies outside the mathematical scope of this dissertation.

### 11.3 Explicit Limitations of the CAC Framework

The CAC framework is explicitly speculative at the level of Assumption A1: it treats CAPI as the sole input to civilizational acceleration beyond physical capital, ignoring governance quality, geopolitical stability, demographic structure, and natural resource constraints. The value θ = 1.5 is chosen to illustrate the mathematical argument, not because it has been estimated from data. The capital stock K is notated but not formalized in its own dynamical equation. Competitive race dynamics — in which two nations racing toward a civilizational goal may under some game-theoretic conditions innovate faster than a cooperative program — are not incorporated into the current framework. These limitations are active research frontiers, not defects in the existing architecture.

