Appendix A: Complete Axiomatic System and Foundational Proofs

A.1 Formal Statement of All Assumptions with Contestability Analysis

Assumption A1 (Dimensional Completeness)

Formal Statement: There exists a finite set of dimensions $\mathcal{D} = \{C, E, S, R, \Lambda\}$ such that the AI strategic capacity $\Psi(N,t)$ of any nation-state $N$ at time $t$ satisfies:

\Psi(N,t) = F(C(N,t), E(N,t), S(N,t), R(N,t), \Lambda(N,t); \kappa_N) + \varepsilon(N,t)

where $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon(N,t)|\mathcal{D}] = 0$ and $\text{Var}[\varepsilon(N,t)]/\text{Var}[\Psi] \leq \delta$ for some small $\delta > 0$.

Contestability: The assumption asserts that no omitted dimension contributes systematically to $\Psi$ beyond white noise. The testable implication is that the fixed effects $\kappa_N$ estimated across nations with similar observable dimensions should be statistically indistinguishable. Systematic variation in $\kappa_N$ correlated with institutional quality metrics constitutes a rejection of A1 and motivates dimensional expansion.

Relaxation Path: Generalized CAPI with $k$-dimensional extension:

\Psi(N,t) = \kappa_N \cdot \prod_{i=1}^k X_i^{\alpha_i} \cdot \exp\left(\sum_{j=k+1}^m \beta_j Z_j\right)

---

Assumption A2 (Multiplicativity)

Formal Statement: The production functional $F: \mathbb{R}_+^5 \to \mathbb{R}_+$ satisfies:

F(x_1, \ldots, x_i = 0, \ldots, x_5) = 0 \quad \forall i \in \{1,\ldots,5\}

and

\frac{\partial^2 \ln F}{\partial \ln x_i \partial \ln x_j} = 0 \quad \forall i \neq j

The first condition establishes essentiality of all dimensions; the second establishes multiplicative separability.

Proof of Necessity: Without semiconductor access ($S = 0$), no competitive AI accelerator can be manufactured or procured. The compute capacity $C$ is then identically zero for any training run exceeding the capability of non-accelerator hardware. By the definition of frontier AI capability, this implies $\Psi = 0$. This holds regardless of $E, R, \Lambda$ values. Therefore $F$ cannot be additive.

Contestability: The essentiality condition may be too strong. A nation with $S = 0$ could theoretically access compute via cloud services (though export controls increasingly restrict this). The proper mathematical representation may be $S \to 0^+$ with $\Psi$ continuous but rapidly decreasing, rather than $S = 0 \implies \Psi = 0$.

---

Assumption A3 (Constant Elasticities)

Formal Statement: For all $t$ in the estimation horizon $\mathcal{T} = [T_0, T_1]$:

\frac{\partial \ln \Psi}{\partial \ln X_i} = \alpha_i \quad \text{constant}

The testable null hypothesis is $H_0: \alpha_i(t) = \alpha_i$ for all $t \in \mathcal{T}$, tested via Andrews (1993) sup-Wald statistic:

\sup_{\tau \in [\tau_0, 1-\tau_0]} W(\tau) \quad \text{where} \quad W(\tau) = T \cdot \frac{(RSS_0 - RSS_1(\tau))}{RSS_1(\tau)}

with $RSS_0$ the restricted sum of squares and $RSS_1(\tau)$ the unrestricted sum allowing a break at fraction $\tau$ of the sample.

Relaxation: If $H_0$ is rejected, specify $\alpha_i(t) = \alpha_i^{(0)} + \alpha_i^{(1)} t + \alpha_i^{(2)} \mathbb{I}(t > t_{\text{break}})$ with $t_{\text{break}}$ estimated via least squares over candidate break dates.

---

Assumption A4 (Weak Separability)

Formal Statement: The five dimensions can be partitioned into $G$ groups $\{\mathcal{G}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{G}_G\}$ such that the marginal rate of substitution between any two dimensions within group $\mathcal{G}_g$ is independent of quantities in any other group $\mathcal{G}_h$, $h \neq g$. For the baseline CAPI, $G = 5$ (complete separability), implying:

\frac{\partial}{\partial X_k}\left(\frac{\partial \Psi / \partial X_i}{\partial \Psi / \partial X_j}\right) = 0 \quad \forall k \notin \{i,j\}

This is automatically satisfied under multiplicativity since:

\frac{\partial \Psi / \partial X_i}{\partial \Psi / \partial X_j} = \frac{\alpha_i X_j}{\alpha_j X_i}

which depends only on $X_i$ and $X_j$.

---

Assumption A5 (Smooth Dynamics with Identified Discontinuities)

Formal Statement: For each dimension $X_i$, there exists a finite set of known discontinuity times $\mathcal{J}_i = \{t_{i,1}, \ldots, t_{i,m_i}\}$ such that:

dX_i(t) = g_i(X(t), t) dt \quad \text{for } t \notin \mathcal{J}_i


\Delta X_i(t_{i,j}) = J_{i,j}(X(t_{i,j}^-)) \quad \text{for } t_{i,j} \in \mathcal{J}_i

where $g_i$ is Lipschitz continuous and $J_{i,j}$ is a known jump function. The full jump specification is provided in Appendix C.

