A.2 Theorem 1: Existence and Uniqueness of CAPI

Theorem A.1: Under Assumptions A1–A5, there exists a unique functional form for the Civilizational AI Power Index up to a multiplicative constant.

Proof: By A1, $\Psi = F(C,E,S,R,\Lambda)$ where $F: \mathbb{R}_+^5 \to \mathbb{R}_+$ is the production functional. By A2 (multiplicative separability), $F$ factorizes as $F = \kappa \cdot \prod_{i=1}^5 f_i(X_i)$. The constant elasticity condition A3 requires $f_i(X_i) = X_i^{\alpha_i}$ for each dimension (this follows from solving the differential equation $\partial \ln f_i / \partial \ln X_i = \alpha_i$). Therefore:

\Psi = \kappa \cdot C^\alpha E^\beta S^\gamma R^\delta \Lambda^\varepsilon

The normalization $\alpha + \beta + \gamma + \delta + \varepsilon = 1$ follows from the requirement that $\Psi$ be homogeneous of degree 1 in the five dimensions (doubling all inputs doubles strategic capacity, a defining property of any well-constructed index). Uniqueness: any alternative representation $F'$ satisfying A1–A4 must coincide with this form up to the multiplicative constant, since the factorization and constant elasticity differential equations have unique solutions. $\square$

---

A.3 Theorem 2: Invariance Under Dimensional Rescaling

Theorem A.2: The CAPI ratio $\text{CAPI}(N_1,t)/\text{CAPI}(N_2,t)$ is invariant under any common rescaling of the measurement units of the five dimensions.

Proof: Let $\tilde{X}_i = \lambda_i X_i$ be rescaled measurements for dimension $i$, with $\lambda_i > 0$. Then:

\widetilde{\text{CAPI}}(N) = \kappa \cdot \prod_i (\lambda_i X_i)^{\alpha_i} = \kappa \cdot \left(\prod_i \lambda_i^{\alpha_i}\right) \cdot \prod_i X_i^{\alpha_i}

The ratio becomes:

\frac{\widetilde{\text{CAPI}}(N_1)}{\widetilde{\text{CAPI}}(N_2)} = \frac{\kappa_1}{\kappa_2} \cdot \frac{\prod_i \lambda_i^{\alpha_i}}{\prod_i \lambda_i^{\alpha_i}} \cdot \frac{\prod_i X_{1i}^{\alpha_i}}{\prod_i X_{2i}^{\alpha_i}} = \frac{\text{CAPI}(N_1)}{\text{CAPI}(N_2)}

The scale factors cancel identically. This property is essential for meaningful international comparison: the CAPI ratio does not depend on whether compute is measured in EFLOPS or PFLOPS, energy in GW or MW, etc. $\square$

