Appendix B: Complete Proofs of All Propositions and Theorems

B.1 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Compute-Energy Coupling Equation)

Proposition: For $N(t)$ active AI accelerators, per-chip throughput $\phi$ FLOP/s, utilization $\eta \in (0,1]$, per-chip power $P_{GPU}$ Watts, and PUE coefficient $\mu \geq 1$:

E_{\text{demand}}(t) = \frac{C(t) \cdot P_{GPU} \cdot \mu}{\eta \cdot \phi}

Proof:

Compute capacity is defined as $C(t) = \eta \cdot N(t) \cdot \phi$. Solving for $N(t)$:

N(t) = \frac{C(t)}{\eta \cdot \phi}

Total energy demand equals number of chips multiplied by per-chip power consumption, multiplied by facility overhead:

E_{\text{demand}}(t) = N(t) \cdot P_{GPU} \cdot \mu = \frac{C(t)}{\eta \cdot \phi} \cdot P_{GPU} \cdot \mu

Substituting the definitions yields the stated result. $\square$

Corollary B.1 (Energy Intensity of Compute): The energy intensity $\rho = E/C = P_{GPU} \cdot \mu / (\eta \cdot \phi)$ measured in Watts per FLOP/s is a hardware-generation-specific constant. For GB200 NVL72 systems with $\phi_{BF16} \approx 180 \times 10^{15}$ FLOP/s per 72-GPU unit, $P_{GPU} \approx 1000$W per GPU (72 kW per unit), $\mu \approx 1.1$ for liquid-cooled facilities, $\eta \approx 0.85$:

\rho \approx \frac{72000 \times 1.1}{0.85 \times 180 \times 10^{15}} \approx 5.18 \times 10^{-13} \text{ W/(FLOP/s)}

---

B.2 Proof of Proposition 3.2 (National Capability Scaling)

Proposition: Under Chinchilla-optimal training, the AI capability of a nation with total compute budget $C_{national}$ and software efficiency multiplier $\Lambda$ satisfies $Q(N,t) \propto C_{national}(t)^{0.5} \cdot \Lambda(t)$.

Proof:

Hoffmann et al. (2022) establish that for a single training run with compute budget $C_0$, the compute-optimal model achieves capability $Q \propto C_0^{0.5}$.

Let the nation possess aggregate compute $C_{national}$, which can be allocated across multiple training runs. Under compute-optimal allocation, $C_{national} = \sum_j C_0^{(j)}$ where each $C_0^{(j)}$ produces capability $Q^{(j)} \propto (C_0^{(j)})^{0.5}$.

The software ecosystem multiplier $\Lambda$ operates through two channels: (1) algorithmic efficiency reduces the effective compute required per unit of capability gain; (2) training infrastructure optimization improves realized utilization $\eta$. In log-space, the effective compute available for capability generation is:

\ln C_{\text{eff}} = \ln C_{national} + 2\ln \Lambda

where the factor of 2 arises because capability scales as $C_{\text{eff}}^{0.5}$, so a multiplicative increase in $\Lambda$ is equivalent to a $\Lambda^2$ increase in compute. Therefore:

Q \propto C_{\text{eff}}^{0.5} \propto (C_{national} \cdot \Lambda^2)^{0.5} = C_{national}^{0.5} \cdot \Lambda

The first-order approximation is exact in the neighborhood of current operating points. $\square$

Corollary B.2 (Substitution Elasticity): The elasticity of substitution between compute and ecosystem depth is:

\sigma_{C,\Lambda} = \left.\frac{d\ln(C/\Lambda)}{d\ln(\partial Q/\partial \Lambda / \partial Q/\partial C)}\right|_{Q \text{ const}} = \infty

at the point of the approximation, indicating perfect substitutability in the first-order sense — a 10% increase in $\Lambda$ perfectly substitutes for a 20% decrease in $C$ at constant $Q$. This is the mathematical basis for the claim that algorithmic innovation can compensate for hardware deficits, though the empirical validity of this elasticity requires $\theta$ estimation.

---

B.3 Proof of Proposition 5.1 (Growth Vector Dominance)

Proposition: The nation with higher inner product $\vec{\alpha} \cdot \vec{g}(t)$ grows its CAPI faster regardless of current absolute position.

Proof:

From the CAPI definition:

\ln \text{CAPI}(N,t) = \ln \kappa_N + \sum_{i=1}^5 \alpha_i \ln X_i(t)

Differentiating with respect to time:

\frac{d}{dt}\ln \text{CAPI} = \sum_{i=1}^5 \alpha_i \frac{\dot{X}_i}{X_i} = \vec{\alpha} \cdot \vec{g}(t)

Let $r(t) = \text{CAPI}_1(t)/\text{CAPI}_2(t)$. Then:

\frac{d}{dt}\ln r(t) = \vec{\alpha} \cdot (\vec{g}_1(t) - \vec{g}_2(t))

The sign of the growth rate differential determines the direction of change in the ratio regardless of its current level. Specifically, if $\vec{\alpha} \cdot \vec{g}_1(t) > \vec{\alpha} \cdot \vec{g}_2(t)$, then $\dot{r}/r > 0$ and Nation 1 pulls ahead even if currently behind. $\square$

---

B.4 Proof of Proposition 6.1 (Sensitivity of Convergence Time)

Proposition: For $r = \text{CAPI}_{US}/\text{CAPI}_{CN}$ and $\Delta g = g_{CN} - g_{US}$:

\frac{\partial T^*}{\partial (\Delta g)} = -\frac{\ln r}{(\Delta g)^2}

Proof:

Starting from the deterministic convergence equation:

\text{CAPI}_{US} \cdot e^{g_{US} T^*} = \text{CAPI}_{CN} \cdot e^{g_{CN} T^*}

\frac{\text{CAPI}_{US}}{\text{CAPI}_{CN}} = e^{(g_{CN} - g_{US})T^*} = e^{\Delta g \cdot T^*}

\ln r = \Delta g \cdot T^*

T^* = \frac{\ln r}{\Delta g}

Taking the partial derivative:

\frac{\partial T^*}{\partial (\Delta g)} = \frac{\partial}{\partial (\Delta g)}\left(\ln r \cdot (\Delta g)^{-1}\right) = -\frac{\ln r}{(\Delta g)^2}

For $r > 1$ (US advantage), $\ln r > 0$, so the derivative is strictly negative: increases in the growth differential reduce convergence time. The quadratic dependence on $\Delta g$ in the denominator establishes the extreme sensitivity claimed in the main text. $\square$

Corollary B.3 (Variance Amplification): If $\widehat{\Delta g}$ is an estimator of $\Delta g$ with standard error $s_{\Delta g}$, the first-order approximation to the variance of $\widehat{T^*}$ is:

\text{Var}[\widehat{T^*}] \approx \left(\frac{\partial T^*}{\partial (\Delta g)}\right)^2 \text{Var}[\widehat{\Delta g}] = \frac{(\ln r)^2}{(\Delta g)^4} \cdot s_{\Delta g}^2

For $r = 2.07$, $\Delta g = 0.010$, and $s_{\Delta g} = 0.003$: $\text{Var}[\widehat{T^*}] \approx (7270)^2 \cdot (0.003)^2 \approx 476$ years², giving a standard error of approximately 21.8 years. The 95% confidence interval spans from approximately 29 to 115 years — wider than the point estimate itself, demonstrating its practical uselessness.

