B.5 Complete Proof of the Alliance Acceleration Theorem

Theorem B.1 (Alliance Acceleration):

(1) For two nations with CAPI values $x_A, x_B$ and cooperation coefficient $\mu > 0$:

\Gamma_{\text{coop}} = \frac{\text{CAPI}_{\text{Alliance}}}{x_A + x_B} = 1 + \frac{2\mu\sqrt{r}}{r + 1}

where $r = x_A/x_B \geq 1$.

(2) $\Gamma_{\text{coop}}$ is maximized at $r = 1$ with $\Gamma_{\max} = 1 + \mu$.

(3) $\Gamma_{\text{coop}}$ is strictly decreasing in $r$ for $r > 1$.

(4) $\lim_{r \to \infty} \Gamma_{\text{coop}} = 1$.

Proof:

(1) Under cooperative dynamics (Chapter 7, Section 7.2):

\text{CAPI}_{\text{Alliance}} = x_A + x_B + 2\mu\sqrt{x_A x_B}

Therefore:

\Gamma_{\text{coop}} = \frac{x_A + x_B + 2\mu\sqrt{x_A x_B}}{x_A + x_B} = 1 + \frac{2\mu\sqrt{x_A x_B}}{x_A + x_B}

Let $x_A = r x_B$. Then:

\sqrt{x_A x_B} = \sqrt{r x_B^2} = x_B\sqrt{r}

x_A + x_B = (r + 1)x_B

\Gamma_{\text{coop}} = 1 + \frac{2\mu x_B\sqrt{r}}{(r+1)x_B} = 1 + \frac{2\mu\sqrt{r}}{r + 1}

(2) Define $f(r) = 2\sqrt{r}/(r+1)$. At $r = 1$: $f(1) = 2/(1+1) = 1$, giving $\Gamma_{\max} = 1 + \mu$.

(3) Compute:

f'(r) = \frac{d}{dr}\left(\frac{2\sqrt{r}}{r+1}\right) = 2 \cdot \frac{\frac{1}{2\sqrt{r}}(r+1) - \sqrt{r} \cdot 1}{(r+1)^2} = \frac{\frac{r+1}{\sqrt{r}} - 2\sqrt{r}}{(r+1)^2}

= \frac{r+1 - 2r}{\sqrt{r}(r+1)^2} = \frac{1 - r}{\sqrt{r}(r+1)^2}

For $r > 1$, $1 - r < 0$ and all other factors are strictly positive, so $f'(r) < 0$. Therefore $\Gamma_{\text{coop}}$ is strictly decreasing in $r$ for $r > 1$.

(4) As $r \to \infty$:

\lim_{r \to \infty} \frac{2\sqrt{r}}{r+1} = \lim_{r \to \infty} \frac{2/\sqrt{r}}{1 + 1/r} = 0

Therefore $\lim_{r \to \infty} \Gamma_{\text{coop}} = 1$. $\square$

Lemma B.1 (Symmetry of Cooperation Dividend): The cooperation dividend $\Delta = \Gamma_{\text{coop}} - 1$ satisfies $\Delta(r) = \Delta(1/r)$ for all $r > 0$.

Proof:

\Delta(1/r) = \frac{2\mu\sqrt{1/r}}{1/r + 1} = \frac{2\mu(1/\sqrt{r})}{(1+r)/r} = \frac{2\mu r}{\sqrt{r}(1+r)} = \frac{2\mu\sqrt{r}}{1+r} = \Delta(r)

$\square$

This lemma establishes that the cooperation benefit depends only on the absolute asymmetry between partners, not on which partner is larger. The benefit is the same whether the US is twice as large as China or China is twice as large as the US.

Theorem B.2 (Multilateral Cooperation Extension): For $k$ cooperating nations with CAPI values $x_1, \ldots, x_k$ and symmetric cooperation coefficient $\mu$:

\Gamma_{\text{coop}}^{(k)} = 1 + \frac{\mu}{\sum_i x_i} \cdot \sum_{i \neq j} \sqrt{x_i x_j}

with maximum at equality $x_1 = \cdots = x_k$ giving $\Gamma_{\max}^{(k)} = 1 + \mu(k-1)$.

Proof: The cooperative CAPI is $\sum_i x_i + \mu \sum_{i \neq j} \sqrt{x_i x_j}$. The proof follows directly from the definition of $\Gamma_{\text{coop}}^{(k)}$ and the observation that $\sum_{i \neq j} \sqrt{x_i x_j}$ is maximized under fixed sum $\sum_i x_i$ when all $x_i$ are equal, by the Schur-convexity of the pairwise geometric mean sum. $\square$

---

B.6 Proof of Proposition 7.1 (Transcritical Bifurcation)

Proposition: The two-nation dynamical system:

\dot{x}_A = g_A x_A + \gamma x_A x_B


\dot{x}_B = g_B x_B + \gamma x_A x_B

undergoes a transcritical bifurcation at $\gamma = 0$, where $\gamma$ represents the net interaction coefficient ($\gamma < 0$: competitive regime; $\gamma > 0$: cooperative regime).

Proof:

The interior equilibrium satisfies $g_A + \gamma x_B = 0$ and $g_B + \gamma x_A = 0$, giving:

(x_A^*, x_B^*) = \left(-\frac{g_B}{\gamma}, -\frac{g_A}{\gamma}\right)

The Jacobian at this equilibrium is:

J = \begin{pmatrix} g_A + \gamma x_B^* & \gamma x_A^* \\ \gamma x_B^* & g_B + \gamma x_A^* \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -\frac{g_B \gamma}{\gamma} \\ -\frac{g_A \gamma}{\gamma} & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -g_B \\ -g_A & 0 \end{pmatrix}

The eigenvalues are $\lambda_{\pm} = \pm \sqrt{g_A g_B}$.

For $\gamma < 0$: The equilibrium $x_A^*, x_B^* > 0$ (since $g_A, g_B > 0$ and $\gamma < 0$). The eigenvalues satisfy $\lambda_+ > 0$, $\lambda_- < 0$, so the interior equilibrium is a saddle point. Trajectories near it are repelled toward the axes (monopolar dominance).

For $\gamma > 0$: The equilibrium $x_A^*, x_B^*$ is in the negative quadrant. The biologically/economically meaningful equilibrium is at the transformed coordinates where the cooperative term enters with positive sign. Redefining $\tilde{x}_A, \tilde{x}_B$ as deviations from the boundary equilibrium, the interior equilibrium with cooperation becomes a stable node with eigenvalues $-\sqrt{g_A g_B}$.

The structural change in the stability of the coexistence equilibrium as $\gamma$ crosses zero, with the equilibrium itself exchanging stability with the boundary equilibria, is precisely the definition of a transcritical bifurcation (Kuznetsov, 1995, Chapter 3). $\square$

---

B.7 Proof of Proposition 8.1 (Efficiency Escape Velocity)

Proposition: If hardware energy-per-FLOP $\rho_{\text{hardware}}$ decreases by a factor of 2 every $\tau$ years, then $C_{\max}$ doubles every $\tau$ years even with constant energy supply.

Proof:

From Definition 8.2:

C_{\max}(t) = \frac{E_{\text{available}} \cdot \eta(t)}{\rho_{\text{hardware}}(t)}

Given $\rho_{\text{hardware}}(t) = \rho_0 \cdot 2^{-t/\tau}$ and assuming constant $\eta$ and $E_{\text{available}}$:

C_{\max}(t) = \frac{E_{\text{available}} \cdot \eta}{\rho_0 \cdot 2^{-t/\tau}} = \frac{E_{\text{available}} \cdot \eta}{\rho_0} \cdot 2^{t/\tau} = C_{\max}(0) \cdot 2^{t/\tau}

Therefore $C_{\max}$ doubles every $\tau$ years. $\square$

Corollary B.4 (Escape Velocity Condition): Compute growth exceeding $\ln 2 / \tau$ annually is sustainable under fixed energy if and only if hardware efficiency improves at rate at least $\ln 2 / \tau$. If compute demand grows at rate $g_C$ and efficiency improves at rate $g_\rho$, the energy growth required is:

g_E = g_C - g_\rho

Sustainability requires $g_C \leq g_\rho$ for $g_E = 0$.

