C.2 First Passage Time Distribution Derivation

Problem: Derive the distribution of $T^* = \inf\{t > 0: \text{CAPI}_{CN}(t) \geq \text{CAPI}_{US}(t)\}$ under the full jump-diffusion dynamics.

Approach: Let $Z_t = \ln(\text{CAPI}_{US}(t)/\text{CAPI}_{CN}(t))$. Under the baseline GBM dynamics for both nations:

dZ_t = (g_{US} - g_{CN} - (\sigma_{US}^2 - \sigma_{CN}^2)/2) dt + \sigma_{US} dW_t^{(US)} - \sigma_{CN} dW_t^{(CN)}

= \mu_Z dt + \sigma_Z dW_t^{(Z)}

where $\sigma_Z^2 = \sigma_{US}^2 + \sigma_{CN}^2 - 2\rho\sigma_{US}\sigma_{CN}$ and $\rho$ is the correlation between the two Wiener processes.

The first passage time for $Z_t$ to hit zero starting from $Z_0 = \ln r > 0$ follows an inverse Gaussian distribution (without jumps):

f_{T^*}(t) = \frac{Z_0}{\sigma_Z \sqrt{2\pi t^3}} \exp\left(-\frac{(Z_0 + \mu_Z t)^2}{2\sigma_Z^2 t}\right)

with expectation $\mathbb{E}[T^*] = Z_0/|\mu_Z|$ (for $\mu_Z < 0$, i.e., $g_{CN} > g_{US}$) and variance $\text{Var}[T^*] = Z_0 \sigma_Z^2/|\mu_Z|^3$.

With jumps: The first passage time distribution must be computed numerically via Monte Carlo simulation, as closed-form expressions exist only for special cases (Kou and Wang, 2003). The simulation protocol is specified in Section C.4.

---

C.3 Jump Parameter Calibration: Lee-Mykland Detection

Algorithm C.1 (Lee-Mykland Jump Detection):

For a high-frequency return series $r_t = \ln(X_t/X_{t-1})$:

1. Compute the realized bipower variation:

BPV_t = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=t-M+2}^{t} |r_i||r_{i-1}|

where $M$ is the window length (typically $M = \sqrt{n}$ for $n$ observations per year).

2. The jump test statistic at time $t$:

\mathcal{L}_t = \frac{r_t}{\sqrt{BPV_t}}

3. Under the null of no jump at time $t$, $\mathcal{L}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1/M)$ asymptotically.
4. Reject the null at significance level $\alpha$ if:

|\mathcal{L}_t| > \Phi^{-1}(1 - \alpha/2) \cdot \sqrt{1/M}

where $\Phi^{-1}$ is the standard normal quantile function.

5. For detected jumps, the jump size is estimated as $\hat{J}_t = r_t$ at rejection times.

This procedure is applied to weekly chip shipment data, monthly policy announcement frequency, and quarterly benchmark scores to populate the jump database.

