G.2 Variance Decomposition for T* Uncertainty

Sources of uncertainty:

1. Measurement error in dimensional levels: $\text{Var}[\ln r] = \sum_i \alpha_i^2 \text{Var}[\ln r_i]$
2. Estimation error in exponents: $\text{Var}[\hat{T}^*|\text{data}] \approx (\partial T^*/\partial \boldsymbol{\alpha})' \text{Cov}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}) (\partial T^*/\partial \boldsymbol{\alpha})$
3. Estimation error in growth rates: $\text{Var}[\hat{T}^*|\text{data}] \approx (\partial T^*/\partial \Delta \mathbf{g})' \text{Cov}(\Delta \hat{\mathbf{g}}) (\partial T^*/\partial \Delta \mathbf{g})$
4. Model uncertainty: Variance across alternative specifications (log-linear vs. translog vs. extended coupling)
5. Fundamental uncertainty: Stochastic volatility and jump risk inherent in the geopolitical system

The total variance decomposition (under squared error loss):

\text{Var}[T^*] = \mathbb{E}[\text{Var}[T^*|\text{data}]] + \text{Var}[\mathbb{E}[T^*|\text{data}]]

where the first term captures posterior uncertainty given model and data, and the second term captures across-model and across-prior uncertainty.

Under heuristic priors with plausible uncertainty magnitudes, the dominant term is fundamental uncertainty (5), followed by growth rate estimation error (3), with measurement error (1) and exponent estimation error (2) contributing less than 10% each to total variance. This decomposition confirms that the primary scientific priority is stochastic modeling (Chapter 9) and Monte Carlo simulation (Phase III), not more precise point estimation of current levels or exponents.

